   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Мог бы ему сказать правду.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Right, let's take their exchange slowly and unpack the Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Совесть, дай поспать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Have a conscience, let me sleep.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'conscience' before landing on 'sleep a bit'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Лёва, что тебе надо?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Lyova, what do you want?' The grammar packs 'Lyova' and 'at' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вы дома не ночевали.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You did not come home last night.' Chef Viktor brings out the contrast between 'you' and 'home' in a few short words. Lyova answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Мы с мамой волнуемся.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Mom and I are worried about you.' The meaning hinges on 'we' working together with 'to worry' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Где вы?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Where are you?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'where' before landing on 'you'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Я в ресторане ночевал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I spent the night at the restaurant.' The '-l' ending on the verb meaning 'spent the night' places the action in the masculine past tense. Chef Viktor answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Сейчас отошел ненадолго в эту...",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Right now I just stepped out briefly to the...' The grammar packs 'any minute' and 'this' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "А что вы там делаете?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what are you doing over there?' Lyova chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'are doing' to keep a polite distance.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Какое дело делаю?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What business am I tending to?' Notice how Russian expresses 'what' and 'I make' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Lyova answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Скоро буду.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I will be back soon.' The grammar packs 'soon' and 'will' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ведешь себя как ребенок.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You are acting like a child.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'behave' and finishing on 'child'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ну, Лёва, взрослый парень.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Come on, Lyova is a grown man.' Lyova brings out the contrast between 'come on' and 'Lyova' in a few short words. Chef Viktor answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Мог бы ему сказать правду.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You could have just told him the truth.' The meaning hinges on 'could' working together with 'truth' in spoken Russian. Now let's listen to the entire scene again without interruption.",
